(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-hemostasis-quiz"]={"title":"Hemostasis & Blood Coagulation — Physiology Quiz","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Red blood cells are rich in carbonic anhydrase. Why is this enzyme important for gas transport?","choices":["It keeps hemoglobin's iron in the ferric state so it can bind oxygen","It lets most CO₂ be carried in the blood as bicarbonate","It allows the red cell to generate ATP without mitochondria","It buffers the plasma directly by producing lactic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃, which dissociates so most CO₂ travels as bicarbonate (also the body's major extracellular buffer). (A) is wrong — oxygen-binding iron must stay ferrous, and the enzyme doesn't set iron's state. (C) is wrong — RBC ATP comes from glycolytic enzymes. (D) is wrong — the enzyme handles CO₂/bicarbonate, not lactic acid."},{"q":"How does the biconcave disc shape of a red blood cell aid its function?","choices":["It maximizes mitochondrial packing for ATP production","It increases the number of heme groups the cell can hold","Its redundant membrane lets the cell deform to pass through capillaries","It stops the cell from ever being trapped and destroyed in the spleen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The biconcave shape provides excess ('redundant') membrane relative to volume, so the cell can bend and squeeze through capillaries narrower than itself. (A) is wrong — mature RBCs have no mitochondria. (B) is wrong — heme number tracks hemoglobin content, not shape. (D) is wrong — aged, stiffened cells are in fact removed in the spleen."},{"q":"Aside from carrying oxygen, what is another major role of hemoglobin?","choices":["It acts as an excellent acid-base buffer","It is the main storage form of iron in plasma","It carries the majority of CO₂ as carbaminohemoglobin","It converts bilirubin into excretable bile pigments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hemoglobin is a very effective acid-base buffer in addition to transporting oxygen. (B) is wrong — iron is stored as ferritin/hemosiderin. (C) is wrong — most CO₂ travels as bicarbonate, not bound to hemoglobin. (D) is wrong — bilirubin is made by macrophages from the porphyrin ring, not by hemoglobin."},{"q":"Platelets have no nucleus. What is the direct consequence of this?","choices":["They are unable to store calcium for clotting","They cannot adhere to exposed collagen","They cannot contract during clot retraction","They cannot reproduce"],"answer":3,"exp":"Without a nucleus, platelets cannot reproduce. (A) is wrong — platelets do store Ca²⁺. (B) is wrong — they adhere to collagen, aided by von Willebrand factor. (C) is wrong — they contract using actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin."},{"q":"What gives platelets their contractile ability, used later in clot retraction?","choices":["Actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin","Osmotic swelling followed by collapse","Beating of surface cilia","ATP-powered flagellar movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Platelets contain actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin (actomyosin), giving them true contractile capability. (B), (C), and (D) describe mechanisms platelets do not use — there is no ciliary, flagellar, or purely osmotic contraction."},{"q":"Platelets enter the circulation through which process?","choices":["Budding directly from vascular endothelial cells","Fragmentation of megakaryocytes","Division of circulating monocytes","Final maturation of reticulocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Platelets are cell fragments released by megakaryocytes in the bone marrow. (A), (C), and (D) name unrelated cells — endothelium, monocytes, and reticulocytes (which mature into red cells) do not produce platelets."},{"q":"The hematocrit ('packed cell volume') measures which of the following?","choices":["Grams of hemoglobin per 100 mL of blood","The number of red blood cells per cubic millimeter","The oxygen carried per gram of hemoglobin","The percentage of blood volume made up of packed red cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hematocrit is the fraction of blood volume occupied by packed red cells (normally ~40–45%). (A) describes hemoglobin concentration, (B) the RBC count, and (C) oxygen-carrying capacity — all distinct measurements."},{"q":"How many oxygen molecules can a single hemoglobin molecule carry, and why?","choices":["Two — one for each beta chain","Sixteen — four for each of the four chains","Four — one for each iron atom","Eight — one for each globin chain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each hemoglobin has four heme groups, each with one iron atom that binds one O₂ molecule — four O₂ molecules (eight oxygen atoms) per hemoglobin. (D) confuses the four chains with eight molecules; (A) and (B) miscount the heme/iron units."},{"q":"In which oxidation state must hemoglobin's iron remain to transport oxygen?","choices":["Ferrous (Fe²⁺); ferric iron cannot carry oxygen","Ferric (Fe³⁺); ferrous iron cannot carry oxygen","Either state carries oxygen equally well","Iron is not directly involved in oxygen binding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxygen is carried only when iron is ferrous (Fe²⁺); oxidation to ferric (Fe³⁺) yields methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen. (B) reverses the states, (C) is false, and (D) is wrong because iron is the oxygen-binding site."},{"q":"Why is methemoglobin unable to support oxygen transport?","choices":["It binds oxygen so tightly it never releases it","Its iron is in the ferric state and cannot carry oxygen","It has lost the globin chains needed for cooperativity","It precipitates and ruptures the red cell membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Methemoglobin contains ferric (Fe³⁺) iron, which cannot bind and transport oxygen. (A) is wrong — the problem is failure to carry O₂, not to release it. (C) is wrong — globin chains are intact. (D) describes a different process."},{"q":"What is the chain composition of the predominant adult hemoglobin (hemoglobin A)?","choices":["Four alpha chains","Two alpha and two gamma chains","Two alpha and two beta chains","Two beta and two delta chains"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hemoglobin A, the main adult form, has two alpha and two beta chains. (B) describes fetal-type pairing, and (A) and (D) are combinations not found in normal adult hemoglobin A."},{"q":"Why do differences among globin chain types matter functionally?","choices":["They change how many iron atoms each molecule contains","They determine the red cell's lifespan","They set the activity of carbonic anhydrase","They alter the oxygen-binding affinity of hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Different globin chains give hemoglobin variants different oxygen-binding affinities. (A) is wrong — each chain still carries one heme/iron. (B) and (C) are unrelated to globin chain identity."},{"q":"How is oxygen bound to hemoglobin's iron?","choices":["By an irreversible covalent bond to the globin chain","By a loose, reversible bond at a coordination site of iron","By an ionic bond, so it is carried as ionic oxygen","By binding to the porphyrin ring, independent of iron"],"answer":1,"exp":"Oxygen binds loosely and reversibly to one of iron's coordination bonds and is carried as molecular (not ionic) oxygen, allowing loading in the lungs and unloading in tissues. (A) is wrong — binding is reversible; (C) is wrong — oxygen stays molecular; (D) is wrong — iron is the binding site."},{"q":"In what form is iron transported through the blood plasma?","choices":["Bound to transferrin","Enclosed within ferritin","As free ionic iron","Within hemosiderin granules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Plasma iron is carried loosely bound to transferrin, which can release it to any tissue cell. (B) and (D) are intracellular storage forms, and (C) is wrong — free ionic iron is not the normal transport form."},{"q":"What is the main intracellular storage form of iron?","choices":["Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin","Ferritin, formed when iron combines with apoferritin","Apotransferrin, the plasma iron carrier"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inside cells, iron combines with apoferritin to form ferritin, the principal storage form. (A) and (D) are transport-related proteins, and (B) is a copper-carrying protein, not an iron store."},{"q":"Where is the largest share of the body's iron located?","choices":["Stored as ferritin in the liver","Within hemoglobin","Within myoglobin","Bound to circulating transferrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Most body iron (~65%) is in hemoglobin. Liver ferritin stores, myoglobin, and transferrin-bound iron each hold much smaller fractions."},{"q":"A low plasma transferrin level (poor iron transport) tends to produce which result?","choices":["Enlarged, macrocytic red cells","Iron overload deposited in tissues","Increased oxygen affinity of the hemoglobin molecule","Hypochromic red cells containing too little hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"exp":"With too little transferrin to deliver iron, red cells are made with less hemoglobin than normal — hypochromic anemia. (A) reflects B12/folate problems; (B) is the opposite of poor transport; (C) is unrelated."},{"q":"After hemoglobin is broken down, what happens to its porphyrin ring?","choices":["It is recycled directly into new hemoglobin","It is stored as hemosiderin in macrophages","It is converted, step by step, into bilirubin","It is excreted unchanged by the kidneys"],"answer":2,"exp":"Macrophages convert the porphyrin ring stepwise into bilirubin, which is released to the blood and secreted in bile. (A) and (B) apply to the iron portion (reused or stored); (D) is wrong — the ring is chemically processed first."},{"q":"What does it mean that hematopoietic stem cells are 'pluripotent'?","choices":["They can produce only red blood cells","They can give rise to several different cell types","They have already committed to a single lineage","They are mature cells already circulating in blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pluripotent means capable of giving rise to several different cell types — here, all blood cell lines. (A) and (C) describe committed/limited cells, and (D) is wrong — stem cells are not mature circulating cells."},{"q":"Hematopoiesis is driven by which two general classes of signals?","choices":["Erythropoietin and thrombopoietin only","Oxygen and carbon dioxide gradients","Vitamin B12 and folate","Growth inducers and differentiation inducers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hematopoiesis is driven by growth inducers (e.g., interleukin-3) and differentiation inducers. (A) names specific hormones, not the general categories; (B) and (C) are conditions/nutrients, not inducer classes."},{"q":"How does hematopoiesis shift in response to hypoxia versus infection?","choices":["Hypoxia drives white-cell output; infection drives red-cell output","Both conditions drive red-cell production equally","Hypoxia drives red-cell production; infection drives white-cell production","Neither condition changes hematopoiesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hematopoiesis adapts: hypoxia stimulates erythropoiesis (more red cells for oxygen delivery), while infection/inflammation stimulates white-cell production. (A) reverses these; (B) and (D) ignore the selective response."},{"q":"From the last month of gestation through adulthood, where does blood cell production mainly occur?","choices":["Bone marrow","The yolk sac","The liver and spleen","The lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"By late gestation and throughout adult life, the bone marrow is the primary site of hematopoiesis. The yolk sac (earliest weeks) and liver/spleen (mid-gestation) are earlier sites, and lymph nodes are not the main producers."},{"q":"Where is the majority of erythropoietin produced?","choices":["The bone marrow","The liver","The spleen","The kidney"],"answer":3,"exp":"About 90% of erythropoietin is made in the kidney (roughly 10% in the liver). The bone marrow is EPO's target, not its main source, and the spleen is not a major producer."},{"q":"What is the primary physiological stimulus for erythropoietin release?","choices":["Hypoxia","A rise in blood pH","High iron stores","Increased blood viscosity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Low tissue oxygen (hypoxia) is the key trigger for EPO release. (B), (C), and (D) do not drive EPO; excessive viscosity is a consequence the system tries to avoid, not a stimulus."},{"q":"Which molecule links low oxygen to increased erythropoietin gene transcription?","choices":["Thrombomodulin","Hypoxia-inducible factor-1 (HIF-1)","Carbonic anhydrase in the tubule","Antithrombin III"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hypoxia raises HIF-1, which binds hypoxia-response elements in the EPO promoter to increase EPO transcription. (A) and (D) are coagulation regulators, and (C) is a CO₂-handling enzyme — none control EPO transcription."},{"q":"The relationship between tissue oxygenation, EPO, and red cell mass is an example of what kind of control?","choices":["A positive feedback loop","Feed-forward amplification","An open-loop system","A negative feedback loop"],"answer":3,"exp":"More red cells improve oxygenation, which then reduces the hypoxic drive for EPO — classic negative feedback. (A) would be self-amplifying (the opposite), and (B)/(C) do not describe this self-correcting system."},{"q":"Why do individuals without functioning kidneys typically become anemic?","choices":["The liver overproduces EPO and suppresses the marrow","Residual EPO (mostly hepatic) is too little for normal red-cell production","The bone marrow is destroyed without a renal signal","Iron can no longer be absorbed at all without a hormone from the kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney makes ~90% of EPO; the ~10% residual (mainly liver) is insufficient, so hematocrit falls toward 23–25%. (A) is wrong — hepatic EPO is too low, not excessive; (C) is wrong — the marrow isn't destroyed; (D) misstates iron absorption."},{"q":"Why are vitamin B12 and folate essential for red blood cell production?","choices":["They form the heme ring of hemoglobin","They carry iron into the mitochondria","They are required for DNA synthesis during rapid cell proliferation","They directly stimulate release of erythropoietin from the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both vitamins are needed to make thymidine triphosphate and therefore DNA, which rapidly dividing red-cell precursors require. (A) is wrong — heme synthesis is a separate pathway; (B) describes transferrin's role; (D) is wrong — they don't trigger EPO."},{"q":"Why can reticulocytes still synthesize hemoglobin even though they are nearly mature?","choices":["They still contain a fully functional nucleus","They retain mitochondria and centrioles","They retain some endoplasmic reticulum and mRNA","They store erythropoietin internally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reticulocytes keep a small amount of endoplasmic reticulum and mRNA, allowing continued hemoglobin synthesis. (A) is wrong — they have no nucleus; (B) and (D) name structures/substances not responsible for this synthesis."},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of events in hemostasis?","choices":["Platelet plug → vascular constriction → blood clot → healing and recanalization","Vascular constriction → platelet plug → blood clot → healing and recanalization","Blood clot → platelet plug → vascular constriction → healing","Vascular constriction → blood clot → platelet plug → healing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hemostasis proceeds as vascular constriction, then a platelet plug, then a blood clot, then healing/recanalization. The other orders misplace one or more steps — constriction comes first and the clot forms after the platelet plug."},{"q":"Which mechanism is most important for constricting large blood vessels after injury?","choices":["Myogenic contraction from direct damage to the vessel wall","Thromboxane A2 released by platelets","Sympathetic nervous reflexes triggered by local pain fibers","Local autacoids from traumatized tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"For large vessels, direct injury triggers myogenic contraction, the most important constriction mechanism. Thromboxane A2 (B) matters most for small vessels, while nervous reflexes (C) and autacoids (D) contribute but aren't dominant for large vessels."},{"q":"Thromboxane A2 released from platelets is especially important for which action?","choices":["Dissolving the fibrin clot once healing begins","Directly activating the intrinsic pathway","Inhibiting platelet aggregation to limit the clot","Constricting smaller blood vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thromboxane A2 from platelets is particularly important for constricting smaller vessels. (A) describes fibrinolysis, (B) is unrelated to TXA2, and (C) is the opposite — TXA2 promotes, not inhibits, platelet activation."},{"q":"Why do platelets stick to injured vessel areas but not to normal, intact endothelium?","choices":["Intact endothelium constantly releases thromboxane A2 to attract platelets","Platelets can bind only to nucleated cells","Their surface glycoproteins repel intact endothelium but adhere to exposed collagen","Intact endothelium lacks tissue factor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Platelet surface glycoproteins repel normal intact endothelium yet adhere to injured endothelium and exposed collagen. (A) and (D) misattribute the selectivity, and (B) is false — platelets bind collagen and matrix, not nucleated cells specifically."},{"q":"What is the role of von Willebrand factor in forming the platelet plug?","choices":["It converts circulating prothrombin into thrombin","It helps platelets adhere to exposed collagen","It cross-links fibrin monomers into a mesh","It inhibits platelets to keep the plug small"],"answer":1,"exp":"Von Willebrand factor bridges platelets to collagen, facilitating adhesion. (A) is the job of prothrombin activator, (C) is factor XIII's role, and (D) is the opposite of vWF's pro-adhesive function."},{"q":"Activated platelets secrete ADP and thromboxane A2. What do these substances accomplish?","choices":["They activate additional platelets through positive feedback","They dissolve the platelet plug as it is forming","They convert fibrinogen into fibrin","They inhibit vascular constriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"ADP and thromboxane A2 recruit and activate more platelets, a positive-feedback loop that builds the plug. (B) is the opposite effect, (C) is thrombin's action, and (D) is wrong — these promote hemostasis."},{"q":"Which step is the rate-limiting step of blood coagulation?","choices":["Conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin","Cross-linking of fibrin by factor XIII","Activation of plasminogen to plasmin","Formation of prothrombin activator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Forming prothrombin activator is the rate-limiting step; once formed, it rapidly converts prothrombin to thrombin. (A) and (B) occur downstream, and (C) is part of clot breakdown, not formation."},{"q":"What does prothrombin activator do?","choices":["It converts fibrinogen directly into fibrin strands","It converts prothrombin to thrombin in the presence of calcium","It cross-links adjacent fibrin fibers","It activates plasminogen to plasmin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Prothrombin activator, with Ca²⁺, converts prothrombin to thrombin. (A) is thrombin's downstream action, (C) is factor XIII's role, and (D) belongs to tissue plasminogen activator in fibrinolysis."},{"q":"How does thrombin act on fibrinogen?","choices":["It cleaves small peptides from fibrinogen, forming fibrin monomers that polymerize","It directly cross-links fibrinogen molecules into a stable insoluble mesh","It converts fibrinogen back into prothrombin","It degrades fibrinogen into soluble fragments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thrombin removes small peptides from fibrinogen to create fibrin monomers, which polymerize into fibrin fibers. (B) is factor XIII's cross-linking step, (C) reverses the pathway, and (D) describes plasmin's degradative action."},{"q":"What is the function of factor XIII (fibrin-stabilizing factor)?","choices":["It converts prothrombin into active thrombin","It initiates the extrinsic pathway","It covalently cross-links fibrin, strengthening the clot","It dissolves the clot after healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Factor XIII, activated by thrombin, covalently cross-links fibrin monomers and fibers, so the clot is weak without it. (A) is prothrombin activator's role, (B) is tissue factor's role, and (D) describes plasmin."},{"q":"How does thrombin promote clot extension through positive feedback?","choices":["By inactivating factor V to slow the reaction","By acting on prothrombin and other factors to generate still more thrombin","By dissolving fibrin at the clot's edge","By blocking the recruitment of any additional platelets to the site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thrombin feeds back to produce more thrombin and more prothrombin activator, extending the clot. (A) is the opposite — thrombin activates factor V; (C) and (D) would limit the clot, not extend it."},{"q":"Clot retraction depends on which cells, acting through what mechanism?","choices":["Platelets contracting via actin, myosin, and thrombosthenin","Red cells contracting by osmotic shrinkage","No cells at all — the retraction is purely spontaneous","White cells engulfing and compacting fibrin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Platelets bound to fibrin contract using actin/myosin/thrombosthenin, tightening the clot; retraction will not occur without platelets. (B), (C), and (D) are incorrect — red cells, spontaneous action, and white cells do not drive retraction."},{"q":"What initiates the extrinsic pathway of coagulation?","choices":["Blood contacting collagen or a glass surface","Activation of factor XII","Thrombin cleaving fibrinogen","Release of tissue factor from traumatized tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"The extrinsic pathway begins when injured tissue releases tissue factor (factor III). (A) and (B) start the intrinsic pathway, and (C) is a later, common-pathway event."},{"q":"In the extrinsic pathway, tissue factor forms a complex that activates which clotting factor?","choices":["Factor XII","Factor VII (converting it to VIIa)","Factor IX","Factor XIII in the plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tissue factor acts as a high-affinity receptor for factor VII, activating it to VIIa; the TF–VIIa complex then activates factor X. (A) and (C) belong to the intrinsic pathway, and (D) is the fibrin-stabilizing factor."},{"q":"What triggers the intrinsic pathway of coagulation?","choices":["Release of tissue factor from injured tissue","Thrombin cleaving fibrinogen","Contact of blood and factor XII with collagen or a wettable surface","Binding of von Willebrand factor to circulating platelets"],"answer":2,"exp":"The intrinsic pathway starts when blood/factor XII contacts collagen or a wettable surface (e.g., glass), activating factor XII. (A) initiates the extrinsic pathway, (B) is a common-pathway step, and (D) relates to platelet adhesion."},{"q":"Where do the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge?","choices":["At the activation of factor X (the common pathway)","At the activation of factor XII by collagen","At the release of tissue factor","At the cross-linking of fibrin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Both pathways meet at the activation of factor X, entering the common pathway toward thrombin and fibrin. (B) and (C) are pathway-specific starting points, and (D) is a later step after thrombin has acted."},{"q":"Why is the extrinsic pathway able to produce a clot faster than the intrinsic pathway?","choices":["It requires many more sequential enzymatic steps","It does not require calcium","It has fewer steps and can clot in under about 15 seconds","It bypasses factor X entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"The extrinsic pathway is 'explosive,' clotting in as little as ~15 seconds because it has fewer steps; the intrinsic pathway is slower (1–6 minutes). (A) is the opposite, (B) is false (Ca²⁺ is still needed), and (D) is wrong — both pathways activate factor X."},{"q":"How does thrombomodulin help limit clotting?","choices":["It directly digests fibrin fibers","It binds thrombin and activates protein C, which inactivates factors Va and VIIIa","It potentiates antithrombin III","It blocks release of tissue factor from the damaged vessel wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thrombomodulin binds thrombin; the complex activates protein C, which inactivates factors Va and VIIIa, slowing coagulation. (A) describes plasmin, (C) describes heparin's mechanism, and (D) is not thrombomodulin's action."},{"q":"What is the role of antithrombin III in controlling coagulation?","choices":["It combines with and inactivates thrombin not bound to fibrin","It converts circulating prothrombin into thrombin","It cross-links fibrin fibers","It activates the extrinsic pathway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Antithrombin III binds and inactivates the thrombin not already trapped on fibrin, limiting clot spread. (B), (C), and (D) describe pro-coagulant steps — the opposite of antithrombin III's inhibitory role."},{"q":"What is vitamin K's essential role in coagulation?","choices":["It cross-links fibrin monomers into a stable clot","It converts plasminogen into plasmin","It directly activates the platelets arriving at the site of vessel injury","It is needed to carboxylate glutamic acid in prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitamin K is required to carboxylate glutamic acid residues on prothrombin and factors VII, IX, X (and protein C), making them functional. (A) is factor XIII's role, (B) is tPA's role, and (C) is unrelated to vitamin K."},{"q":"How is a clot eventually broken down during healing?","choices":["Thrombin is converted to plasmin, which cross-links fibrin","Fibrinogen is converted to plasmin, which builds new clot","Plasminogen is activated to plasmin, which digests fibrin fibers","Antithrombin is converted to plasmin, which stores iron"],"answer":2,"exp":"Tissue plasminogen activator converts plasminogen (trapped in the clot) to plasmin, which digests fibrin and reopens the vessel. (A), (B), and (D) misidentify plasmin's precursor and function — plasmin comes from plasminogen and degrades, rather than builds, the clot."}]};
